## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit rotation across word boundary without per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; cross-word mixing; edge k<64 vs k>=64

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi, lo; } uint128_t;
static inline uint128_t rotl128(uint128_t x, unsigned k){
  k %= 128; uint128_t r;
  if (k < 64){
    r.hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    r.lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
  } else { unsigned kk = k - 64;
    r.hi = (x.lo << kk) | (x.hi >> (64 - kk));
    r.lo = (x.hi << kk) | (x.lo >> (64 - kk));
  }
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test boundary cases k=0, 63, 64, 127?
- Could you adapt to 256-bit using 4x uint64_t without loops?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a branchless, constant-time population-count across 128 bits using only shifts and masks, a common low-level optimization in crypto/signature code.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 64-bit popcount, extended to 128-bit
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example

```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t w) {
  w = w - ((w >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  w = (w & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((w >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  w = (w + (w >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((w * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  unsigned int hi = popcount64(x.hi);
  unsigned int lo = popcount64(x.lo);
  return hi + lo;
}
```

